<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yujiulü Hulü</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 414</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yujiulü Hulü was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> of the Rouran from 410 to 414, succeeding his brother Shelun, the founder of the khaganate. In 414 he was overthrown in a succession struggle within the royal clan, the first of many such disputes that weakened Rouran rule.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Yujiulü Hulü ruled the Rouran, a confederation of nomadic tribes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, with the title Aikugai Khagan, in Rouran Uqaqai Qaγan. His rule is recorded as beginning in May 410. The following year he offered a tribute of three thousand horses to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feng-ba">Feng Ba</a>, ruler of Northern Yan, and asked to marry his daughter, Princess Lelang, probably the daughter of Feng Ba's wife Princess Sun.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feng-ba">Feng Ba</a>'s brother Feng Sufu advised refusing and sending the daughter of one of Feng Ba's concubines instead, but Feng Ba appears to have judged an alliance with the Rouran useful to his state, and in 411 Princess Lelang was married to Yujiulü Hulü.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 414, as he prepared to send one of his own daughters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feng-ba">Feng Ba</a> in marriage, his nephew Yujiulü Buluzhen objected that the girl was still small and would be going far away, and that the daughters of noblemen such as Shuli and Wudeyan ought to go with her. Hulü refused. Buluzhen then told Shuli and the others that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> meant to give their daughters away as a dowry to a distant, alien state, and the noblemen joined him in a conspiracy.</p><p>Warriors hidden behind Hulü's yurts seized him and his daughter at night. Buluzhen was made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a>, with Shuli as his chancellor, and sent the deposed ruler and his daughter to Northern Yan. Feng Ba received Hulü as an honoured guest and, as first planned, took the daughter, Zhaoyi, as a concubine. At Hulü's request he reluctantly gave him an escort home in May 414 under the general Wan Ling, who, according to the account, returned after killing Yujiulü Hulü on the way.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yujiulü Hulü” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
